/-! # The coefficient ideal intersection in a projective symmetric presentation

Source: Stacks Project, Smoothing Ring Maps, Tag 07CE, equation-sequence;
More on Algebra, Tags 07CX and 07D4, specialized to a split symmetric presentation.
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-projective-symmetric-coefficient-intersection)
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u

namespace Algebra

/-- The square of the presentation kernel intersects the coefficient ideal in their product.
Source: Stacks Project, More on Algebra, Tags 07CX and 07D4, as used in the proof of
Smoothing Ring Maps, Tag 07CE, equation-sequence.
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-projective-symmetric-coefficient-intersection)

Proof. Put A = P/I, M = I/I², Q = P[y], H = ker(Q → A[y]), J = ker φ.
The surjection q splits because M is projective. Therefore M and ker q are finite
projective, as direct summands of A^m. Choose a common finite principal-open cover
on which both are free, using finite projective standard free covers and their
pairwise intersections. Lift its defining elements to P. After localization,
symmetric-algebra base change and bases adapted to A^m = M ⊕ ker q identify α
with the coordinate projection A_a[u,v] → A_a[u]. Its kernel is generated by the
complementary variables v, which are a regular sequence on every nonzero member.
Apply the regular-quotient intersection theorem with H ≤ J to get H ∩ J² = HJ
on each such member. Localization preserves the intersections, powers, products
and quotient in (H ∩ J²)/HJ. This quotient is a C-module, where C = Sym_A M,
since J annihilates it: J(H ∩ J²) ≤ JH. Its localized modules vanish, so the
unit-ideal cover forces the quotient to vanish globally. A zero member has zero
modules and needs no regular-sequence assertion. Thus H ∩ J² = HJ globally.
For I = P all rings over A are zero; H = J = Q and the equality is immediate.
For m = 0, surjectivity gives M = 0 and the same argument has no complementary
variables. -/
theorem projective_symmetric_coefficient_intersection
    (P : Type u) [CommRing P] (I : Ideal P) (m : ℕ)
    [Module.Projective (P ⧸ I) I.Cotangent]
    (q : (Fin m → P ⧸ I) →ₗ[P ⧸ I] I.Cotangent) (hq : Function.Surjective q)
    (α : MvPolynomial (Fin m) (P ⧸ I) →ₐ[P ⧸ I] SymmetricAlgebra (P ⧸ I) I.Cotangent)
    (hαX : ∀ j, α (MvPolynomial.X j) =
      SymmetricAlgebra.ι (P ⧸ I) I.Cotangent (q (Pi.single j 1)))
    (φ : MvPolynomial (Fin m) P →+* SymmetricAlgebra (P ⧸ I) I.Cotangent)
    (hφeq : φ = α.toRingHom.comp (MvPolynomial.map (Ideal.Quotient.mk I))) :
    RingHom.ker (MvPolynomial.map (Ideal.Quotient.mk I) (σ := Fin m)) ⊓
        (RingHom.ker φ) ^ 2 =
      RingHom.ker (MvPolynomial.map (Ideal.Quotient.mk I) (σ := Fin m)) *
        RingHom.ker φ := by sorry

end Algebra
